@h1 Migration from LaTeX h1@

You tolerate LaTeX's ceremony because the output is worth it. Wit offers a
trade: keep the semantic structure you love — sections, figures, citations,
your own macros — and drop the preamble, the package hunt, and the compile
toolchain. This page maps the LaTeX you know onto Wit, then tells you plainly
where Wit stops. The honesty matters: Wit is not a typesetting engine, and this
page will not pretend otherwise.

@h2 The relief, first h2@

No @@@code \documentclass code@@@. No @@@code \usepackage code@@@ dependency hell.
No multi-pass build. A Wit document @em is em@ its content, and the theme is
batteries-included. A file of prose becomes a styled PDF with one command:

@@@pre
wit build paper.wit -o paper.pdf
pre@@@

That's the whole toolchain. The document you write is the document you get.

@h2 The mapping h2@

@h3 Sectioning h3@

@@@code \section{X} code@@@ becomes @@@code @h1 X h1@ code@@@;
@@@code \subsection{X} code@@@ becomes @@@code @h2 X h2@ code@@@, and so on down to
@@@code @h6 code@@@.

@h3 Text emphasis h3@

@@@code \textbf{x} code@@@ becomes @@@code *x* code@@@ (a single asterisk);
@@@code \emph{x} code@@@ and @@@code \textit{x} code@@@ become @@@code _x_ code@@@ (a
single underscore). A line break, @@@code \\ code@@@, becomes @@@code @br code@@@.

@h3 Lists h3@

@@@code \begin{itemize} code@@@ becomes @@@code @ul code@@@ and
@@@code \begin{enumerate} code@@@ becomes @@@code @ol code@@@, each holding
@@@code @li code@@@ items in place of @@@code \item code@@@.

@h3 Figures h3@

@@@code \begin{figure} … \caption{…} \end{figure} code@@@ becomes a
@@@code @figure code@@@ wrapping an @@@code @img code@@@ and a
@@@code @figcaption code@@@:

@@@pre
@figure
  @img |src ./plot.png| |alt Signal over time|
  @figcaption Figure 1. The keeper's log. figcaption@
figure@
pre@@@

@h3 Tables h3@

@@@code \begin{tabular} code@@@ becomes a @@@code @table code@@@ node — rows
inline, or generated from data:

@@@pre
@table |rows [[Site, Status], [Dunmore Head, operational]]| |caption Stations|
pre@@@

@h3 Macros h3@

@@@code \newcommand{\chapter}[2]{…} code@@@ becomes a definition with captured
parameters, @@@code ::name:: code@@@ interpolation, and a @@@code ... code@@@ body
slot:

@@@pre
#chapter ||number, title||
@h2 ::number:: — ::title:: h2@
...
chapter#

@chapter |number I| |title The Keeper|
For eleven days the lamp had burned untended.
chapter@
pre@@@

@h2 Citations, done the way you wish LaTeX did h2@

This is the feature LaTeX authors miss most in lighter tools, and Wit does it
natively — no @code .bib code@ file, no BibTeX pass. Define a citation template
once, name your sources against it, and cite by @em idea em@ in prose.

Define the template as a component:

@@@pre
#cite ||author, title, year||
::author:: (::year::), _::title::_.
cite#

@cite |author Simone Weil| |title Gravity and Grace| |year 1952|
pre@@@

That renders @strong Simone Weil (1952), Gravity and Grace. strong@ For an in-text
citation, name a source once and drop it into a sentence — two words, and it
expands in place:

@@@pre
#weil: Simone Weil, Gravity and Grace (1952) !!

Attention, as @weil argued, is the rarest form of generosity.
pre@@@

A bibliography can even gather its own entries: each additive
@@@code +#bibliography code@@@ appends to the list, so contributions scattered
across a manuscript collect themselves in order.

@@@pre
#bibliography: Weil, Gravity and Grace (1952). !!
+#bibliography: Berger, Ways of Seeing (1972). !!

@bibliography
pre@@@

@h2 The honest gaps h2@

Wit trades typesetting control for simplicity. Where that trade costs you, here it
is, stated plainly.

@h3 There is no math h3@

Wit has @strong no built-in math typesetting strong@ — no @@@code $…$ code@@@, no
equation environment. You have two workable paths. Pre-render the math elsewhere
and embed it as an image, @@@code @img |src ./eq.svg| |alt x squared plus y squared| code@@@;
or pass it through with @@@code @node(type math) … node@ code@@@ for a custom
renderer that knows how to typeset it. There are @@@code @sub code@@@ and
@@@code @sup code@@@ nodes for simple subscripts and superscripts, but they are not
a math system — see the gotcha below.

@h3 It is not a full typesetting engine h3@

There is no fine page-layout control — no floats, no @@@code \vspace code@@@, no
custom margins, no multi-column typesetting. A Wit PDF is the default HTML theme
paginated by a headless browser, not a TeX run. Wit optimises for @strong semantic
structure and clean default output strong@, not press-ready typography. If your
work lives or dies on kerning and float placement, keep LaTeX for that job.

@h3 Bibliography styles are yours to write h3@

The citation template above is exactly that — a template @em you em@ define. It
is powerful and completely under your control, but there is no @code .bst code@
ecosystem of ready-made styles to switch between. You get the mechanism; the style
is your prose.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li @strong Reaching for @@@code $x^2$ code@@@. strong@ There is no inline math. @@@code @sup code@@@ handles a superscript only with a word boundary before it: @@@code 2 @sup nd sup@ code@@@ gives @code 2 code@ with a raised @code nd code@, but @@@code x@sup 2 sup@ code@@@ breaks — a letter directly before @code @ code@ suppresses the opener. You cannot set a tight @code x² code@ this way; use a math image for anything real. li@
@li @strong Expecting @@@code \\ code@@@ to break a line. strong@ Use @@@code @br code@@@ for an explicit line break. li@
@li @strong Putting a comma inside a record field value. strong@ LaTeX tolerates commas inside macro arguments; Wit reads a comma in a @@@code { … } code@@@ record as a field separator, so @@@code { title - Attention, Perception } code@@@ fails with @code E_MALFORMED_RECORD code@. Escape it as @@@code \, code@@@, restructure, or use a form-fill body where commas are safe. li@
@li @strong Assuming a preamble is required. strong@ It isn't. Delete the mental @@@code \documentclass code@@@ — a bare file of prose already builds. li@
ul@

@h2 See also h2@

For the citation and bibliography mechanics in depth, start with
@a |href /docs/start/mental-model/| the mental model a@ and
@a |href /docs/start/nodes/| Nodes and core vocabulary a@. To build a full document
step by step, follow @a |href /docs/start/tutorial/| the tutorial a@. When a build
errors, @a |href /docs/start/troubleshooting/| Troubleshooting a@ decodes it — the
record-comma error above is covered there.
